 you cannot define multiple different Pod definitions inside a single Kubernetes ReplicaSet template section.

 A ReplicaSet's spec.template field takes only one PodTemplateSpec object, which acts as a single blueprint used to stamp out identical copies (replicas) of that exact Pod.

Depending on what you are trying to achieve, Kubernetes handles multiple workloads in the following ways:

• Multiple identical Pods (Scaling): If you want multiple copies of the same Pod, set spec.replicas to the desired number (e.g., replicas: 3). The ReplicaSet will create three identical Pods from that single template.

• Multiple containers inside one Pod (Sidecars/Helpers): If you have two containers that must run together on the same node, share storage, and talk over localhost, define multiple items under the spec.template.spec.containers list inside that single Pod template.

• Different types of Pods (Separate Workloads): If you need to run completely different Pods that scale independently (such as a frontend web server and a backend database), 
create two separate ReplicaSets or Deployments, each with its own template.

